            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with R5 insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



Adding insulation to the outside of a house is a significant renovation, so we suggest that it be done when you replace the siding on the outside of your house. Overall, the siding on your house is in good shape, and your walls are already well insulated, so I would not place a high priority on this upgrade at this time. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $415&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

Since there is no access to your attic, I assumed that it has 12 inches (R40) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation for a total of R60 in your attic.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $140&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. You would not qualify for any rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program as your attic insulation is already greater than R35.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilings>
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a natural gas-fired on-demand hot water heater. I have modelled replacing this with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Because you already have an on-demand hot water heater, I do not recommend that you upgrade your water heater until it has reached the end of its service life. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $110&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current natural gas fired boiler was originally rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing gas boiler and also upgrade the hot water heater to a direct vent unit, the current combustion air duct can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing natural gas boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $395&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;HRV Use:&lt;/b&gt;

When I visited your house, you told me that you did not use your HRV. The HRV is used to exchange indoor air with outside air, and preheats the outside air coming in so that your heating system does not work as hard. Running your HRV will provide better ventilation in your home, and will decrease the mold and condensation on your windows. 



Good ventilation is important to ensure a healthy indoor environment. I strongly recommend that you speak to a contractor in Inuvik who installs and maintains HRVs to make sure that yours is in good condition, and to learn how yours will best operate. I have also included some information from AEA about HRVs in the north. 



&lt;b&gt;Windows:&lt;/b&gt;

Your windows are already triple paned, hinged windows that appear to have a Low E coating on them. As such I do not recommend replacing any of your windows at this time. However, should you ever need to replace a window for any other reason such as physical damage, look for ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3 that are triple paned, double Low E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Foundation Insulation:&lt;/b&gt;

When I visited your home, I discussed some possibilities for your crawlspace. At this time, I do not recommend adding any insulation to your crawlspace walls. As your house is built on permafrost, adding insulation will trap heat under the house, causing your house to shift.



I do recommend installing venting to prevent moisture from your dryer and other exhaust fans to build up in the crawl space. 



&lt;b&gt;ECM Pumps:&lt;/b&gt;

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Additional Comments: &lt;/b&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Inuvik&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik.

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh

Natural gas - $37.21 per GJ

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $1.52 per litre

Wood - $472.50 per cord 

Pellets - $11.25 per bag



